  {"type": "problems", "h2": "Air conditioner problems we repair in Toronto homes",
   "lead": "Toronto lots and Toronto houses give air conditioners a hard life. Here is what goes wrong and how we put it right.",
   "items": [
    {"problem": "Warm air on the first 30°C day of the year", "solution": "The unit sat all winter and a weak run capacitor or pitted contactor finally gave up, or the charge is low from a slow leak. We test the capacitor under load, check pressures and amp draw, and replace what actually failed. Both parts ride on the truck."},
    {"problem": "The condenser is wedged in a metre-wide side yard and keeps overheating", "solution": "An outdoor unit needs clear space to dump heat. Pressed against the neighbour's wall with a fence on the other side, it runs hot, trips on high pressure and shortens the compressor's life. We clean the coil, restore what clearance we can, and if the spot is hopeless we quote moving it to a wall bracket or the rear yard."},
    {"problem": "The third floor is sticky even with the AC running flat out", "solution": "Century semis were ducted for heat, and the few supply runs that reach the top floor cannot carry enough cold air. We check static pressure and dampers first. Often the honest fix is a single ductless head upstairs so the central system can stop fighting a battle it cannot win."},
    {"problem": "Your AC still uses R-22 and a top-up costs a fortune", "solution": "R-22 refrigerant is no longer produced, so every top-up on an older Toronto unit costs more than the last and the leak is still there. We will repair a small leak if the unit is sound, but we will also show you what a replacement or a cold-climate heat pump costs, because that math has changed."},
    {"problem": "The breaker trips every time the compressor starts", "solution": "A compressor pulling high starting amps, a dying capacitor or a tired breaker in a 60-year-old panel all look the same from the kitchen. We measure the inrush current and inspect the disconnect and panel, with ESA-licensed electrical work if the fix is on that side."},
    {"problem": "Water on the basement floor beside the furnace in July", "solution": "The evaporator coil above your furnace pulls litres of water out of humid Toronto air every day. When the drain line clogs with slime or the trap is missing, that water goes onto the floor. We clear the line, add a trap or a float switch, and check the coil for ice."},
    {"problem": "The ductless head upstairs drips or stops cooling", "solution": "Ductless units in Toronto additions and third floors fail for two main reasons: a plugged condensate drain and a blocked filter or coil. Both are quick. If the problem is the line set or the outdoor unit, a licensed tech leak tests it before adding any refrigerant."},
    {"problem": "The air is cool but the house still feels damp", "solution": "An oversized air conditioner cools the air near the thermostat and shuts off before it has pulled the humidity out. Lake humidity makes it worse. We confirm the sizing against the house and, when you replace, recommend a variable-speed system that runs long and dries the air properly."}]},
