  {"type": "split", "h2": "Three questions we ask before repairing an air conditioner in Toronto", "photo": "sys",
   "html": "<p>Not every AC repair is worth doing, and the answer in Toronto depends on more than the unit's age. We ask three things before we recommend anything.</p><p><strong>What refrigerant is in it?</strong> An R-22 unit with a leak is at the end of the road whatever its age, because the refrigerant is scarce and the leak will not fix itself. An R-410A unit with a capacitor fault has years left.</p><p><strong>Where does the condenser sit?</strong> A unit that has been cooking in a one-metre side yard for 15 years has had a harder life than its age suggests. If the compressor is tired and the spot is bad, replacing it on a wall bracket in a better location pays twice.</p><p><strong>Can the ducts carry the cooling?</strong> If the top floor never gets cold, a new central unit will not fix that. The right plan may be a smaller central system plus a ductless head, or a <a href='/heat-pump/'>heat pump</a> that earns a rebate while it replaces the AC.</p><ul class=\"checklist\"><li>Repair when the unit is under about ten years old and the part is small</li><li>Replace when the quote is a compressor or coil on an old or R-22 unit</li><li>Consider a heat pump whenever the furnace is also past 15 years</li></ul><p>Our <a href='/ac/repair-maintenance/'>AC repair and maintenance page for Ontario</a> covers the annual tune-up in detail, and <a href='/toronto/'>HVAC in Toronto</a> covers the rest of what we do in the city.</p>"},
